<h1>Video 065 — Default constructor</h1>
<h2>Video info</h2>
<p>Title: Core Java With OCJP/SCJP: OOPs(Object Oriented Programming) Part-15 || default constructor</p>
<p>ASR decode (Whisper): Proto-Type / Conceptor = prototype / constructor; norangumine = no-arg meaning; excess modifier = access modifier; so per / Soper / soper = super; piece code / P&#x27;s code = programmer’s code; c is code / C&#x27;s code = compiler-generated code; parent = super(); void test = method named test (not a constructor); this of ten = this(10); KALTO / call to = “call to”; PUST = first; compelty / compil meod = compile-time error; public lawyer Yaman Medan / public void M1 = public void m1(); oper / Twilight Diss = super / this; up e = P (parent class).</p>
<h3>00:04 — Topic: prototype of the default constructor</h3>
<p>Next: what is the prototype of the default constructor?</p>
<p>Compiler will generate the default constructor — what does that generated constructor look like? He writes the points carefully.</p>
<h3>00:32 — Setup: empty class → compiler generates default constructor</h3>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
}</pre>
<p>Programmer wrote no constructor → compiler generates the default constructor. What is its prototype?</p>
<h3>00:49 — Point 1: always a no-arg constructor</h3>
<p>Default constructor is always a no-argument constructor.</p>
<p>But be careful: every no-arg constructor is not a default constructor.</p>
<p>Wrong mental model: people see a no-arg constructor and call it “default.” Example — if the programmer writes:</p>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    Test() {
    }
}</pre>
<p>This is a constructor provided by the programmer. It is not a default constructor.</p>
<p>Compiler-generated constructor = default constructor.</p>
<p>Summary:</p>
<ul><li>Default constructor → always no-arg.</li></ul>
<ul><li>No-arg constructor written by programmer → not called default constructor.</li></ul>
<h3>02:05 — Point 2: access modifier same as class</h3>
<p>What is the access modifier of the default constructor?</p>
<p>Same as the class’s access modifier.</p>
<ul><li>If the class is public → default constructor is public.</li></ul>
<ul><li>If the class is default (package-private) → default constructor is default.</li></ul>
<p>Rule applies for public and default only — because for top-level classes you cannot apply private / protected anyway. So private/protected default-constructor talk is not applicable for top-level classes.</p>
<h3>03:10 — Point 3: body is exactly one line — `super()`</h3>
<p>Does the default constructor contain any code?</p>
<p>Yes — exactly one line:</p>
<p>super();</p>
<p>That is a no-argument call to the superclass constructor.</p>
<p>Default constructor contains only that one line.</p>
<h3>04:12 — Dictation of the three prototype points</h3>
<ol><li>Always a no-arg constructor.</li></ol>
<ol><li>Access modifier of default constructor is exactly same as access modifier of the class (applicable only for public and default).</li></ol>
<ol><li>Contains only one line: super(); — no-arg call to superclass constructor.</li></ol>
<p>Highlight: only one line super();.</p>
<h3>06:55 — Table idea: P’s code vs C’s code</h3>
<p>He works examples as two columns:</p>
<p>Do not confuse “parent” wording in ASR with inheritance here — on the board, the inserted line is super();.</p>
<h3>07:47 — Example 1: empty default class</h3>
<p>P’s code:</p>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
}</pre>
<p>Compiler generates default constructor? Yes — no constructor written.</p>
<p>C’s code (effective):</p>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    Test() {
        super();
    }
}</pre>
<h3>08:31 — Example 2: `public` class</h3>
<p>P’s code:</p>
<pre class='code'>public class Test {
}</pre>
<p>Compiler generates? Yes.</p>
<p>C’s code:</p>
<pre class='code'>public class Test {
    public Test() {
        super();
    }
}</pre>
<p>Access modifier of default constructor matches the class → public Test().</p>
<h3>09:17 — Example 3: method named `test` is not a constructor</h3>
<p>P’s code:</p>
<pre class='code'>public class Test {
    void test() {
    }
}</pre>
<p>Compiler generates default constructor? Yes — because we did not write any constructor.</p>
<p>void test() is a method (has return type). Programmer may not realize that; compiler still generates the default constructor.</p>
<p>C’s code:</p>
<pre class='code'>public class Test {
    public Test() {
        super();
    }

    void test() {
    }
}</pre>
<p>void test() is not the default constructor. The compiler-generated public Test() { super(); } is.</p>
<h3>10:41 — Example 4: programmer wrote empty no-arg constructor</h3>
<p>P’s code:</p>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    Test() {
    }
}</pre>
<p>Will compiler generate a default constructor? No — programmer already wrote a constructor, so compiler is “happy” and does not generate another default constructor.</p>
<p>But compiler still does a second check: did the programmer write the constructor properly?</p>
<p>Rule: the first line inside every constructor should be either `super(...)` or `this(...)`.</p>
<p>If we write nothing, compiler always places super(); as the first statement.</p>
<p>Here programmer wrote a constructor but did not write super or this → compiler inserts super();.</p>
<p>C’s code:</p>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    Test() {
        super();   // inserted by compiler
    }
}</pre>
<p>Important: not generating a new default constructor — generating the missing super(); inside the programmer’s constructor.</p>
<h3>13:05 — Example 5: arg constructor with explicit `super()`</h3>
<p>P’s code:</p>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    Test(int i) {
        super();
    }
}</pre>
<p>Compiler generate any constructor / extra code? No.</p>
<ul><li>Programmer wrote a constructor → no default constructor.</li></ul>
<ul><li>First line is already super() → written properly.</li></ul>
<p>Best style: programmer did everything; compiler generates nothing extra. Code stays as written.</p>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    Test(int i) {
        super();
    }
}</pre>
<h3>14:06 — Example 6: `this(10)` chaining; missing `super` in other ctor</h3>
<p>P’s code:</p>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    Test() {
        this(10);
    }

    Test(int i) {
    }
}</pre>
<p>Checks:</p>
<ol><li>Programmer wrote constructor(s)? Yes → no default constructor generated.</li></ol>
<ol><li>Wrote properly?</li></ol>
<ul><li>First constructor: first line is this(10) → OK (no need to insert super there).</li></ul>
<ul><li>Second constructor: no super / this → compiler places super();.</li></ul>
<p>C’s code:</p>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    Test() {
        this(10);
    }

    Test(int i) {
        super();   // generated by compiler
    }
}</pre>
<h3>16:09 — Board recap of P’s / C’s pairs</h3>
<p>He revisits the table cases (empty class, public class, void test() method case, etc.) with red highlight on compiler-generated parts. Terminology must be clear: programmer’s code vs compiler-generated code.</p>
<h3>20:34 — Theory reminder (already covered)</h3>
<p>The first line inside every constructor should be either `super` or `this`.</p>
<p>If we are not writing anything, compiler always places:</p>
<p>super();</p>
<p>Dictation:</p>
<p>The first line inside every constructor should be either super or this.</p>
<p>And if we are not writing anything, then compiler will always place super();.</p>
<h3>22:04 — Three important cases / conclusions</h3>
<p>Based on that discussion: three important cases about super / this usage.</p>
<h3>22:22 — Case 1: `super` / `this` only as **first** statement</h3>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    Test() {
        System.out.println(&quot;Constructor&quot;);
        super();
    }
}</pre>
<p>Invalid.</p>
<p>Reason / CE:</p>
<pre class='code'>// CE: call to super must be first statement in constructor</pre>
<p>Within a constructor, super(...) or this(...) only in the first line. Anywhere else → compile-time error.</p>
<p>Valid form:</p>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    Test() {
        super();
        System.out.println(&quot;Constructor&quot;);
    }
}</pre>
<p>Live demo of the bad order → same CE message: call to super must be first statement in constructor.</p>
<p>Case 1 dictation: We can take super or this only in the first line of the constructor. If we try anywhere else → compile-time error.</p>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    Test() {
        System.out.println(&quot;Constructor&quot;);
        super();
    }
}
// CE: call to super must be first statement in constructor</pre>
<h3>26:34 — Case 2: either `super` or `this`, not both</h3>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    Test() {
        super();
        this();
    }
}</pre>
<p>First line super() alone would be fine, but second line this() is invalid — super/this only allowed as the first statement, and you can take either super or this, not both simultaneously (only one constructor-call line).</p>
<p>CE style:</p>
<pre class='code'>// CE: call to this must be first statement in constructor
class Test {
    Test() {
        super();
        this();
    }
}
// CE: call to this must be first statement in constructor</pre>
<p>Case 2 dictation: Within the constructor we can take either super or this, but not both simultaneously.</p>
<h3>28:40 — Case 3: only inside a constructor — not inside a method</h3>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    public void m1() {
        super();
        System.out.println(&quot;Hello&quot;);
    }
}</pre>
<p>Invalid.</p>
<p>CE (same family of message):</p>
<pre class='code'>// CE: call to super must be first statement in constructor</pre>
<p>Why: super() / this() are constructor calls. You can use them only inside a constructor. Inside a method you are misusing them.</p>
<p>Purpose reminder:</p>
<ul><li>super(...) → call superclass constructor directly</li></ul>
<ul><li>this(...) → call current class another constructor</li></ul>
<p>You can call a constructor directly (superclass or current class) only from a constructor. From a method → invalid / CE.</p>
<p>Case 3 dictation: We can use super() or this() only inside a constructor. Outside (e.g. in a method) → compile-time error.</p>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    public void m1() {
        super();
    }
}
// CE: call to super must be first statement in constructor</pre>
<p>(Same message even though the real issue is “not in a constructor.”)</p>
<h3>34:36 — Extra line he hammers</h3>
<p>We can call a constructor directly from another constructor only.</p>
<h3>35:19 — Summary of `super()` / `this()` rules (constructor calls)</h3>
<p>Single-line summary:</p>
<p>`super` / `this` (as constructor calls):</p>
<ol><li>Only in constructors</li></ol>
<ol><li>Only in the first line</li></ol>
<ol><li>Only one — not both simultaneously</li></ol>
<h3>37:47 — Difference: `super()` / `this()` vs `super` / `this` keywords</h3>
<p>Interview chance: difference between:</p>
<ul><li>super() / this() → constructor calls — to call superclass / current-class constructors.</li></ul>
<ul><li>super / this → keywords — to refer to superclass / current-class instance members.</li></ul>
<p>Example:</p>
<pre class='code'>class P {
    int x = 100;
}

class C extends P {
    int x = 200;

    public void m1() {
        System.out.println(this.x);   // current class x → 200
        System.out.println(super.x);  // superclass x → 100
    }
}</pre>
<p>this.x → current class instance member.</p>
<p>super.x → superclass instance member.</p>
<p>These are keywords, not constructor calls.</p>
<h3>41:13 — Second conclusion: where can you use each?</h3>
<p>Why except static? super / this refer to instance members. Instance terminology not allowed in static area.</p>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(super.hashCode());
    }
}
// CE: non-static variable super cannot be referenced from a static context</pre>
<p>(Object / parent has hashCode; still illegal from static context.)</p>
<h3>43:47 — Third conclusion: how many times?</h3>
<h3>44:44 — Comparison table (dictation)</h3>
<h3>47:26 — Closing example: `super` in static area</h3>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(super.hashCode());
    }
}
// CE: non-static variable super cannot be referenced from a static context</pre>
<p>Completed: clarity on default-constructor prototype, P’s vs C’s code, and super/this constructor-call rules vs keyword rules.</p>
